          <h1>Sample Article Title: Understanding a Japan-Specific System</h1>

          <p class="article-meta">
            Housing · Published May 4, 2026 · Last updated May 4, 2026
          </p>

          <p>
            This is a short lead paragraph. It should explain who the article is for,
            what problem it solves, and what the reader will understand after reading it.
            For example, this article explains a common Japan-specific housing issue in
            clear English for foreign professionals, researchers, students, and globally
            mobile readers.
          </p>

          <h2>Quick summary</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>This section gives the reader the main conclusion first.</li>
            <li>Use short bullet points for practical information.</li>
            <li>Clarify what readers should check before making a decision.</li>
            <li>Link to official sources when the topic depends on rules, laws, or institutions.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Why this matters</h2>

          <p>
            Many Japan-specific systems are difficult to understand because the important
            information is often written in Japanese, scattered across official documents,
            or explained through local practices rather than simple written rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            This article template is designed to explain such topics in a structured way:
            first the basic idea, then the practical meaning, then points to check, and
            finally cautions or next steps.
          </p>

          <h2>Basic terms</h2>

          <p>
            Start by defining the key terms. If the topic involves Japanese words, give the
            Japanese term, a direct translation, and a practical explanation.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Term 1:</strong> Short explanation of the first important term.</li>
            <li><strong>Term 2:</strong> Short explanation of the second important term.</li>
            <li><strong>Term 3:</strong> Short explanation of the third important term.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Practical interpretation</h2>

          <p>
            This section should explain what the topic means in real life. For example,
            if the article is about rental contracts, this is where you explain what the
            clause usually means for tenants, what documents to request, and what should
            be confirmed before signing or paying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid presenting individual legal, tax, financial, immigration, or real estate
            advice. Instead, frame the article as general information and encourage readers
            to confirm important matters with official sources or qualified professionals.
          </p>

          <h2>Checklist</h2>

          <p>
            A checklist is useful for practical articles. It also makes the page easier to
            scan on mobile devices.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Check the official name of the document or procedure.</li>
            <li>Confirm the deadline, fee, and required documents.</li>
            <li>Save screenshots, receipts, emails, and written explanations.</li>
            <li>Ask for clarification if the explanation is unclear.</li>
            <li>Consult an appropriate professional for important individual decisions.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Common mistakes</h2>

          <p>
            This section can explain common misunderstandings. It is especially useful for
            articles aimed at readers who are new to Japan.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Assuming that English summaries always capture the full Japanese meaning.</li>
            <li>Relying on informal explanations without checking the original document.</li>
            <li>Missing deadlines or notice periods because they are written in Japanese.</li>
            <li>Confusing general practice with legally guaranteed rules.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Useful sources to check</h2>

          <p>
            When writing the real article, add links to official or highly reliable sources
            here. For example, government agencies, municipal offices, universities, public
            institutions, or official service providers.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Official source 1</li>
            <li>Official source 2</li>
            <li>Relevant institutional page</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Final note</h2>

          <p>
            This article is intended to provide general information and practical context.
            Rules, fees, procedures, and institutional practices may change, and individual
            situations may differ. Readers should confirm important matters with official
            sources or qualified professionals.
          </p>

